In React, “same object” usually means **same contents**, but React cares about **same reference**.

If you create an object inside the parent render, a **new object is created on every render**:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const user = { name: "Ada" };

  return <Child user={user} />;
}
```

Even though `{ name: "Ada" }` looks the same each time, it is a new object:

```js
{ name: "Ada" } === { name: "Ada" } // false
```

So if `Child` is memoized with `React.memo`, React will see the prop as changed because the object reference changed.

---

## Example

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ user }) {
  console.log("Child render");
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const user = { name: "Ada" };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <Child user={user} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Every time `Parent` renders, this line creates a new object:

```js
const user = { name: "Ada" };
```

So `Child` re-renders.

---

## Fix with `useMemo`

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const user = useMemo(() => {
    return { name: "Ada" };
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <Child user={user} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Now `user` keeps the same reference between renders.

---

## Also memoize the child

`useMemo` alone does not stop a child render. The child also needs to be memoized:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ user }) {
  console.log("Child render");
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});
```

So the usual combo is:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ user }) {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const user = useMemo(() => ({ name: "Ada" }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <Child user={user} />
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Same issue with functions

Functions are also recreated on each render:

```jsx
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Use `useCallback` if the child is memoized:

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething();
}, []);

<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

---

## Important note

By default, when a parent renders, React will call its child components too. To skip child renders, you need:

```jsx
React.memo(Child)
```

And the props must be referentially equal according to React’s shallow comparison.

So the reason is usually:

> You are passing an object with the same values, but not the same identity/reference. React sees it as a changed prop.

Because in React, **when a parent renders, its children are rendered too by default**.

Passing the “same object” only matters if the child is memoized.

### 1. Child re-renders by default

```jsx
function Parent() {
  return <Child obj={someObj} />;
}
```

Every time `Parent` renders, React calls `Child` again.

That is normal.

### 2. `React.memo` can skip child renders

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ obj }) {
  console.log("Child render");
  return <div>{obj.name}</div>;
});
```

Now React will shallow-compare props and skip rendering if props are the same.

### 3. But object identity matters

This creates a **new object every render**:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const obj = { name: "Alice" };

  return <Child obj={obj} />;
}
```

Even though it looks the same, it is not the same reference.

Use `useMemo`:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const obj = React.useMemo(() => ({ name: "Alice" }), []);

  return <Child obj={obj} />;
}
```

Or define it outside the component if it never changes:

```jsx
const obj = { name: "Alice" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child obj={obj} />;
}
```

### 4. Same issue with functions

This creates a new function every render:

```jsx
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Use `useCallback` if needed:

```jsx
const handleClick = React.useCallback(() => {
  doSomething();
}, []);

<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

### 5. Other things that still cause re-render

Even with `React.memo`, the child can re-render if:

- its props change by reference
- its own state changes
- a context it uses changes
- its `key` changes
- React Strict Mode double-invokes renders in development

### Typical fix

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ user, onSelect }) {
  return <div onClick={onSelect}>{user.name}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const user = React.useMemo(() => ({ name: "Alice" }), []);

  const handleSelect = React.useCallback(() => {
    console.log("selected");
  }, []);

  return <Child user={user} onSelect={handleSelect} />;
}
```

In short: **children re-render when parents render unless you memoize the child, and object/function props must keep the same reference for memoization to work.**

In React, a child component **renders whenever its parent renders by default**.

Even if the props “look the same,” React will still call the child function unless you explicitly opt into memoization with `React.memo`.

Example:

```jsx
