Here is the detailed breakdown of why this happens and how to fix it.

### Reason 1: React's Default Behavior
By default, when a parent component re-renders, **all of its children re-render**, regardless of whether their props changed or not. React does not compare props by default because doing so on every render can be expensive.

### Reason 2: Referential Equality in JavaScript
If you are defining the object inside the parent component, a **brand new object is created in memory every single time the parent renders**. 

In JavaScript, objects are compared by their memory reference, not their values. 
`{ id: 1 } === { id: 1 }` is **false**.

So, even if you tell React to check the props, it will see the new object, realize the memory reference has changed, and trigger a re-render.

