Great question! This is one of the most common React performance pitfalls. The root cause is **referential equality**.

## The Problem

In JavaScript, objects (and arrays/functions) are compared **by reference**, not by value:

```js
{ a: 1 } === { a: 1 } // false — two different objects in memory
```

So when your parent re-renders, it creates a **new object literal** on every render — even if the contents are identical. React sees a new reference and concludes the prop changed.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ❌ New object created on every render!
  const config = { theme: "dark", size: "lg" };

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

Even wrapping `Child` in `React.memo` won't help here, because `memo` uses shallow equality (`===`) — and that object reference is always new.

---

## The Fixes

### 1. `useMemo` — memoize the object
```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Same reference unless dependencies change
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: "dark", size: "lg" }), []);

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

### 2. Move the object **outside** the component (if it's static)
```jsx
// ✅ Defined once, never recreated
const config = { theme: "dark", size: "lg" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

### 3. Pass **primitives** instead of an object
```jsx
// ✅ Primitives are compared by value
<Child theme="dark" size="lg" />
```

### 4. Wrap `Child` in `React.memo` + use `useMemo` together
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  // ...
});
```
`React.memo` alone isn't enough — you still need a stable reference from `useMemo`.

---

## Quick Decision Guide

| Situation | Fix |
|---|---|
| Object is static (no deps) | Move it outside the component |
| Object depends on props/state | `useMemo` with correct deps |
| Function prop causing re-renders | `useCallback` (same idea) |
| Complex case | `React.memo` + `useMemo` together |

---

## The Same Problem Applies to Functions & Arrays

```jsx
// ❌ New function reference every render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />

// ✅ Stable reference
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

The golden rule: **anything created inline inside a component body gets a new reference on every render.** Primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`) are safe because they're compared by value.

## The Problem: Object Identity, Not Value Equality

This is one of React's most common gotchas. When you write something like this in the parent:

```jsx
// Parent component
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ❌ This creates a NEW object on every render!
  const config = { theme: "dark", size: "lg" };

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

Even though `config` *looks* the same every time, **React uses `===` (referential equality) to compare props** — not a deep value comparison. Since a new object literal is allocated on every render, `config !== config` across renders, so the child always re-renders.

---

## The Fix: Use `useMemo`

Memoize the object so its reference stays stable across renders:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Same reference as long as dependencies don't change
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: "dark", size: "lg" }), []);

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

---

## Also Wrap the Child in `React.memo`

`useMemo` alone isn't enough — you also need to tell React to *skip re-rendering* the child when props haven't changed:

```jsx
// Child component
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

Without `React.memo`, the child **always** re-renders when the parent does, regardless of props.

---

## The Same Problem Happens with Functions

```jsx
// ❌ New function reference every render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />

// ✅ Stable reference with useCallback
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

---

## Quick Checklist

| Cause | Fix |
|---|---|
| Object literal in render | `useMemo(() => ({...}), [deps])` |
| Array literal in render | `useMemo(() => [...], [deps])` |
| Inline function | `useCallback(() => {}, [deps])` |
| Child not bail-out aware | Wrap child in `React.memo` |

---

## Key Mental Model

> React doesn't ask *"did the data change?"* — it asks *"is this the exact same reference in memory?"*

So the two-part recipe is always:
1. **`useMemo`/`useCallback`** in the parent → stabilize the reference
2. **`React.memo`** on the child → opt into skipping renders when props are stable

